Object.assign(window.deepArticles,{
  "five-power-relations":fivePowerRelationsArticle({
    title:"Bilateral relations of the Five Powers",infoboxKicker:"Ten relationships sustaining the Armed Concert",
    lead:"The bilateral relations of the Five Powers are the ten diplomatic relationships joining the Greater German Reich, the Empire of Japan, the United States, the British Commonwealth, and the Italian-led Latin system. No single alliance, council, or constitutional charter governs the mature international order. Peace instead depends upon overlapping treaties, hotlines, commercial bargains, military estimates, client restraints, and expectations developed separately in every pair.",
    canon:"Recognition of the Five Powers, ten distinct bilateral relationships, absence of a common governing council, Lisbon as the shared postwar baseline, Mid-Atlantic, Tehran, Verona, Halifax, and orbital instruments as later partial settlements, continued limited wars, strategic ownership barriers, space-rescue duties, and an armed concert by the late 1980s are established. A formal Five-Power charter, universal readiness code, permanent secretariat, compulsory court, and complete diplomatic calendar are not established.",
    sections:[
      {id:"members",title:"The Five Powers",html:"<p>The recognized principals are <a href='germany.html'>Germany</a>, <a href='japan.html'>Japan</a>, the <a href='united-states.html'>United States</a>, the <a href='commonwealth.html'>British Commonwealth</a>, and the Italian-led <a href='latin-bloc.html'>Latin system</a>. Their status rests on cumulative industrial, oceanic, financial, imperial, military, and aerospace capability rather than membership in one organization.</p>"},
      {id:"matrix",title:"The bilateral matrix",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Mature character</th><th>Principal restraint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href='germany-japan-relations.html'>Germany–Japan</a></td><td>Regulated détente after strategic rivalry</td><td>Tehran Concord</td></tr><tr><td><a href='germany-united-states-relations.html'>Germany–United States</a></td><td>Atlantic and hemispheric rivalry</td><td>Mid-Atlantic Ridge Accord</td></tr><tr><td><a href='germany-united-kingdom-relations.html'>Germany–United Kingdom</a></td><td>Enduring adversarial restraint</td><td>War-termination logic and incident rules</td></tr><tr><td><a href='germany-italy-relations.html'>Germany–Italy</a></td><td>Post-Axis managed rivalry</td><td>Verona Convention</td></tr><tr><td><a href='japan-united-states-relations.html'>Japan–United States</a></td><td>Pacific strategic competition without prior general war</td><td>Geography, trade, and escalation control</td></tr><tr><td><a href='japan-united-kingdom-relations.html'>Japan–United Kingdom</a></td><td>Postwar hostility and practical accommodation</td><td>Commonwealth defense and maritime rules</td></tr><tr><td><a href='italy-japan-relations.html'>Italy–Japan</a></td><td>Distant pragmatic relations</td><td>Lack of a contested core frontier</td></tr><tr><td><a href='united-states-united-kingdom-relations.html'>United States–United Kingdom</a></td><td>Close cooperation with sovereignty disputes</td><td>Halifax Understanding</td></tr><tr><td><a href='italy-united-states-relations.html'>Italy–United States</a></td><td>Commercial and hemispheric competition</td><td>Local-state sovereignty and investment rules</td></tr><tr><td><a href='italy-united-kingdom-relations.html'>Italy–United Kingdom</a></td><td>Mediterranean rivalry and selective diplomacy</td><td>Route interdependence and localized settlements</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"lisbon",title:"Lisbon inheritance",html:"<p>The 1947 <a href='lisbon-conference.html'>Lisbon settlement</a> ends the long war and recognizes German, Italian, and Japanese victories while preserving an unoccupied Britain and a neutral United States. It establishes the map every later bilateral relationship must either accept, contest indirectly, or manage.</p>"},
      {id:"settlements",title:"Partial settlements",html:"<p>The order develops through instruments narrower than a general peace constitution. The <a href='mid-atlantic-ridge-accord.html'>Mid-Atlantic Ridge Accord</a> bounds German–American expansion; the <a href='tehran-concord.html'>Tehran Concord</a> regulates Germany and Japan; the <a href='verona-convention.html'>Verona Convention</a> restrains Germany and Italy; and the <a href='halifax-understanding.html'>Halifax Understanding</a> defines a practical American–Commonwealth partnership.</p>"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Commerce across rivalry",html:"<p>Trade, licenses, shipping, insurance, technical standards, and space services expand without eliminating protected markets. Strategic ownership remains closed. A company may sell into a rival system while being prohibited from controlling its launch, communications, energy, armaments, or command institutions.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Space as common field and firebreak",html:"<p>Common distress frequencies, approach notification, rescue duties, docking adapters, debris warnings, and protection of inhabited life-support systems create minimum cooperation. Military reconnaissance and armed spacecraft remain legal, making orbit both an arena of rivalry and a place where every dyad has reason to preserve shared infrastructure.</p>"},
      {id:"concert",title:"From relationships to concert",html:"<p>By the late 1980s diplomats speak routinely of the Five Powers. The term does not create collective government. It recognizes that no settlement concerning one of the five can be imposed without its participation and that general war among them lacks a usable political victory.</p>"},
      {id:"limits",title:"Limits of the system",html:"<p>The bilateral order protects great-power survival more reliably than peripheral states. Client coercion, colonial rule, embargoes, covert action, company violence, and finite wars continue. Its achievement is narrower: keeping those conflicts from becoming a war to settle the existence of another great power.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"germany-japan-relations.html",kicker:"Published dyad",label:"Germany–Japan Relations"},{href:"great-power-status.html",kicker:"Membership principle",label:"Great-Power Status"},{href:"great-power-escalation-ladder.html",kicker:"Crisis grammar",label:"Great-Power Escalation Ladder"},{href:"war-termination-doctrine.html",kicker:"Planning restraint",label:"War-Termination Doctrine"},{href:"commercial-detente.html",kicker:"Economic layer",label:"Commercial Détente"},{href:"geneva-orbital-incidents-agreement.html",kicker:"Space rules",label:"Geneva Orbital Incidents Agreement"}],
    facts:[["Recognized principals","Germany · Japan · United States · British Commonwealth · Latin system"],["Possible bilateral pairs","10"],["Universal alliance","None"],["Common founding charter","None"],["Shared postwar baseline","Lisbon settlement · 1947"],["Mature description","Armed Concert"],["Principal restraint","Unusable victory and war termination"],["Strategic ownership","National or bloc-controlled"]]
  }),

  "germany-united-states-relations":fivePowerRelationsArticle({
    title:"Germany–United States relations",eyebrow:"German–American relations · Atlantic and hemispheric rivalry",infoboxKicker:"Neutrality, Iceland, the Atlantic boundary, and bounded competition",
    lead:"Germany–United States relations are the diplomatic, military, commercial, and technical relations between the Greater German Reich and the United States. The United States remains neutral during Germany's victorious long war, leaving the two powers without a direct wartime peace to reconcile. Their later rivalry centers on the Atlantic, the American Hemisphere, overseas clients, industrial competition, and space rather than an ideological commitment to destroy the other state.",
    canon:"American neutrality in the long war, postwar ideological and strategic rivalry, German penetration attempts in the Americas, American interventions, the 1967–68 Iceland Crisis, the 1968 Mid-Atlantic Ridge Accord, continuing intelligence and commercial competition, later investment and technical exchange, and non-allied armed-concert relations are established. Complete ambassador lists, trade totals, intelligence cases, annual force postures, and a comprehensive later bilateral treaty are open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"war",title:"Neutrality during the long war",html:"<p>Washington does not enter the European war. Germany defeats the Soviet Union and forces the Lisbon settlement without fighting a declared war against the United States. The absence of direct combat prevents a Versailles-style bilateral peace while leaving American politics divided over whether neutrality saved the republic or permitted hostile empires to mature.</p>"},
      {id:"postwar",title:"Postwar rivalry",html:"<p>Germany builds the <a href='einheitspakt.html'>Einheitspakt</a> and the United States develops the <a href='american-hemisphere.html'>American Hemisphere</a>. Each treats the other's strategic missions, arms sales, communications, and concession networks as possible preparation for encirclement.</p>"},
      {id:"hemisphere",title:"Competition in the Americas",html:"<p>German military and industrial influence is strongest where local governments seek an alternative to Washington, notably in Argentina and parts of the Andean and Caribbean systems. American interventions in Honduras and Venezuela establish that Washington will use force when foreign technical missions approach oil, communications, bases, or command systems.</p>"},
      {id:"iceland",title:"Iceland Crisis",html:"<p>The <a href='iceland-crisis.html'>Iceland Crisis</a> of 1967–68 brings the two powers closest to direct Atlantic war. German access and northern surveillance confront American Greenland, naval, aerospace, and hemispheric claims. Wolfgang Hitler accepts that operational success cannot answer the problem of a prolonged American war.</p>"},
      {id:"accord",title:"Mid-Atlantic Ridge Accord",html:"<p>The 1968 <a href='mid-atlantic-ridge-accord.html'>Mid-Atlantic Ridge Accord</a> establishes a rough strategic boundary: German Europe faces west to Iceland while the Americas remain principally American. The agreement limits overt deployments without ending commerce, intelligence, companies, clients, or maritime patrols.</p>"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Commerce and investment",html:"<p>Late-century trade expands in machinery, chemicals, computing, transport, consumer goods, and specialized industrial licenses. The World Market Boom encourages German–American investment while blocked-property rules prevent foreign control of strategic firms.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Space and network relations",html:"<p>Both powers operate large independent orbital and lunar systems. Safety, rescue, docking, notification, and data standards create practical cooperation; military reconnaissance, commercial rivalry, and different approaches to networks and corporate jurisdiction preserve competition.</p>"},
      {id:"mature",title:"Mature relationship",html:"<p>By 2000 neither government expects ideological convergence. Each accepts the other as a permanent great power whose defeat would create a larger political problem than the original dispute. Relations remain armed, suspicious, commercially entangled, and negotiable.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...fivePowerRelationsHub,{href:"iceland-crisis.html",kicker:"Formative confrontation",label:"Iceland Crisis"},{href:"mid-atlantic-ridge-accord.html",kicker:"1968 settlement",label:"Mid-Atlantic Ridge Accord"},{href:"german-american-investment-boom.html",kicker:"Commercial opening",label:"German–American Investment Boom"}],
    facts:[["Wartime relationship","No declared German–American war"],["Principal postwar seam","North Atlantic and American Hemisphere"],["Major crisis","Iceland · 1967–68"],["Controlling boundary instrument","Mid-Atlantic Ridge Accord · 1968"],["Formal alliance","None"],["General war","Avoided"],["Commercial relationship","Expanded but ownership-restricted"],["Status by 2000","Permanent strategic competitors"]]
  }),

  "germany-united-kingdom-relations":fivePowerRelationsArticle({
    title:"Germany–United Kingdom relations",eyebrow:"Anglo–German relations · defeat, resistance, and armed restraint",infoboxKicker:"The longest adversarial relationship of the post-Lisbon order",
    lead:"Germany–United Kingdom relations are the diplomatic, military, economic, and social relations between the Greater German Reich and the United Kingdom at the center of the British Commonwealth. The states remain enemies through the long war until Britain accepts the Lisbon settlement without German occupation. Later relations are defined by British resistance identity, German continental predominance, mutual war planning, limited commerce, and shared rules intended to prevent incidents from reopening the war.",
    canon:"War from 1939 to the 1947 Lisbon settlement, British survival without occupation, White Parachute recovery, continuing German and British contingency planning, postwar North Sea and Channel confrontation, British rejection of Germany's European order, limited trade, orbital and rescue cooperation, and declining utility of general war by 2000 are established. Full diplomatic normalization dates, embassy arrangements, trade totals, incident lists, and a comprehensive bilateral settlement are open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"war",title:"Long war",html:"<p>Britain and Germany remain principal enemies from 1939 through 1947. German victory on the continent and in the east does not produce an occupation of the British Isles. Bombardment, submarine war, blockade, colonial loss, and exhaustion instead force a negotiated settlement at Lisbon.</p>"},
      {id:"lisbon",title:"Lisbon and the unoccupied defeat",html:"<p>The <a href='lisbon-conference.html'>Lisbon Conference</a> ends active war while preserving the Crown, Parliament, Royal Navy, and Commonwealth. Britain loses strategic positions and accepts the continental result without recognizing National Socialism as legitimate or abandoning the possibility of national recovery.</p>"},
      {id:"recovery",title:"White Parachute Britain",html:"<p>The <a href='white-parachutes.html'>White Parachute settlement</a> and later reconstruction organize Britain around survival, route security, industrial recovery, and preparation against a final German attack. German power becomes the permanent external reference against which British defense institutions explain themselves.</p>"},
      {id:"frontier",title:"North Sea and Channel frontier",html:"<p>German bases, missiles, submarines, continental air power, and French facilities face British naval, air, missile, and coastal defenses. The frontier is intensely observed but not governed by one grand treaty. Exercises, patrols, intelligence, and warning procedures carry much of the relationship.</p>"},
      {id:"deterrence",title:"Mutual deterrence without atomic weapons",html:"<p>Germany can devastate British ports and cities but cannot define a manageable conquest of the Isles and Commonwealth. Britain can make invasion and occupation ruinously expensive but cannot overturn the continental empire. The war aim, not the absence of military plans, restrains both governments.</p>"},
      {id:"diplomacy",title:"Selective diplomacy",html:"<p>Britain accepts rescue, navigation, orbital, debris, and incident-management rules while refusing a Tehran- or Verona-style recognition of Germany's European leadership. Communication remains necessary precisely because political reconciliation is incomplete.</p>"},
      {id:"commerce",title:"Commerce and technical contact",html:"<p>Shipping, machinery, standards, computing gateways, scientific exchange, tourism, and space services expand late in the century. Strategic firms and command systems remain protected. Commercial accommodation does not erase British memory of defeat or German awareness of British hostility.</p>"},
      {id:"mature",title:"Relations by 2000",html:"<p>Britain retains a grievance but no practical program of reconquest. German invasion plans still lack a usable political objective. Both increasingly prefer competition in trade, standards, clients, and extraterrestrial construction to another war across the Channel.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...fivePowerRelationsHub,{href:"lisbon-conference.html",kicker:"War settlement",label:"Lisbon Conference"},{href:"white-parachutes.html",kicker:"British response",label:"White Parachutes"},{href:"war-termination-doctrine.html",kicker:"Strategic restraint",label:"War-Termination Doctrine"}],
    facts:[["State relationship","Wartime enemies · postwar armed restraint"],["War ended","Lisbon settlement · 1947"],["German occupation of Britain","None"],["Principal frontier","Channel · North Sea · North Atlantic"],["British strategic objective","Commonwealth survival and independence"],["German strategic problem","No governable victory"],["Comprehensive later bilateral treaty","Not established"],["Status by 2000","Adversarial but increasingly bounded"]]
  }),

  "germany-italy-relations":fivePowerRelationsArticle({
    title:"Germany–Italy relations",eyebrow:"German–Italian relations · Axis partnership and managed rivalry",infoboxKicker:"From wartime co-belligerence to the Verona settlement",
    lead:"Germany–Italy relations are the diplomatic, military, economic, and imperial relations between the Greater German Reich and the Kingdom of Italy. The two states enter the divergence as Axis partners and Tripartite Pact signatories, win the long war in connected European and Mediterranean theaters, and then separate into neighboring imperial systems. By the 1960s their former alliance has become a strategic rivalry centered on the Balkans, the French successor states, transit, clients, and technical missions.",
    canon:"Axis and Tripartite alignment, wartime co-belligerence, postwar division into German and Italian systems, rivalry after the Hitler and Mussolini successions, Balkan Missions and Sofia procurement crises, competition over Serbia, Bulgaria, Turkey, and the French states, Italian participation at the Raumhafen, and the late-1985 Verona Convention are established. Exact rupture date, ambassadorial crises, trade totals, intelligence operations, and all implementing instruments remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"axis",title:"Axis partnership",html:"<p>Germany and Italy coordinate war aims through the Axis and <a href='tripartite-pact.html'>Tripartite Pact</a>. Their forces fight different but connected campaigns: Germany dominates the continental and eastern war while Italy leads much of the Mediterranean and Suez struggle.</p>"},
      {id:"victory",title:"Victory and divided empires",html:"<p>The Lisbon settlement confirms both as victors. Germany dominates continental Europe and the eastern empire. Italy controls a Mediterranean and African system whose ports, islands, clients, and routes do not fit inside German command.</p>"},
      {id:"rupture",title:"The post-founder rupture",html:"<p>The deaths and successions of Hitler and Mussolini remove the personal and wartime ties that had disguised incompatible interests. Neither state formally repudiates the alliance tradition at once, but joint planning and automatic political support disappear.</p>"},
      {id:"balkans",title:"Balkan rivalry",html:"<p>Serbian railways, Bulgarian commands, Romanian exercises, Greek and Aegean bases, Turkish passage, and technical missions become recurring disputes. Equipment ownership, adviser presence, data access, and operational authority are treated as separate questions.</p>"},
      {id:"france",title:"The two French systems",html:"<p>Germany protects Northern France and strategic Atlantic facilities while Italy and the Latin system support Occitania and Mediterranean interests. Both oppose an uncontrolled French reunification that might eliminate their respective gains.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Scientific contact amid rivalry",html:"<p>Italy becomes the first foreign state committed to a module at the Orbitaler Raumhafen. Italian personnel work under German station-wide authority while Rome develops the independent Latin Space Community and Stella Maris.</p>"},
      {id:"verona",title:"Verona Convention",html:"<p>The late-1985 <a href='verona-convention.html'>Verona Convention</a> renounces direct aggressive war, requires notification near sensitive fronts, restrains recognized clients, and preserves Italian primacy in Serbia alongside German transit and commercial rights. Bulgaria remains deliberately unresolved.</p>"},
      {id:"after",title:"Relations after 1985",html:"<p>Verona makes rivalry governable without recreating the Axis. Commerce, passage, technical work, and crisis consultation continue beside arms competition, intelligence, and different policies toward the Balkans, France, Turkey, and the Mediterranean.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...fivePowerRelationsHub,{href:"german-italian-rivalry.html",kicker:"Postwar conflict",label:"German–Italian Strategic Rivalry"},{href:"verona-convention.html",kicker:"1985 settlement",label:"Verona Convention"},{href:"italian-raumhafen-module.html",kicker:"Technical bridge",label:"Italian Raumhafen Module"}],
    facts:[["Wartime relationship","Axis partners and co-belligerents"],["Postwar relationship","Neighboring strategic rivals"],["Principal contested region","Balkans"],["French alignment","Northern France versus Occitania"],["Orbital contact","Italian Raumhafen participation"],["Controlling settlement","Verona Convention · late 1985"],["Alliance restored","No"],["Direct aggressive war","Renounced"]]
  }),

  "japan-united-states-relations":fivePowerRelationsArticle({
    title:"Japan–United States relations",eyebrow:"Japanese–American relations · Pacific competition without Pearl Harbor",infoboxKicker:"Avoided wartime collision, Philippine rivalry, and Pacific balance",
    lead:"Japan–United States relations are the diplomatic, military, commercial, and technical relations between the Empire of Japan and the United States. Their defining divergence is the war that does not occur: Japan attacks European colonial systems while avoiding Pearl Harbor, the Philippines, Guam, and Wake. The two powers therefore enter the postwar Pacific as heavily armed competitors without a shared general-war memory or occupation settlement.",
    canon:"Japanese avoidance of war with the United States, preservation of American Pacific possessions, postwar competition over the Philippines and Pacific islands, Japanese support for Philippine independence, American hemispheric and oceanic containment, commercial and investment growth, industrial and space competition, incident-management rules, and absence of a formal alliance are established. Complete crises, trade totals, naval understandings, ambassadors, and the precise post-1985 Pacific settlement remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"before",title:"Relations before the divergence",html:"<p>American restrictions, the war in China, and competing visions of Pacific order produce severe tension. Tokyo nevertheless concludes that war with the United States would endanger the southern campaign and expose the empire to an industrial opponent it cannot usefully conquer.</p>"},
      {id:"avoidance",title:"The war not fought",html:"<p>The Imperial Limited-War Directive excludes attacks on major American possessions. Japan fights Britain and the Netherlands while the United States remains neutral. No Pearl Harbor, island campaign, strategic bombing, occupation, or American-written Japanese constitution enters the relationship.</p>"},
      {id:"postwar",title:"Two Pacific systems",html:"<p>Japan emerges with the Co-Prosperity Sphere and a broad western Pacific perimeter. America retains Hawaii outside the Union, Alaska outside the Union, Guam, Wake, other Pacific positions, and a large two-ocean fleet. Maritime geography creates permanent observation without requiring immediate war.</p>"},
      {id:"philippines",title:"Philippine conflict",html:"<p>The <a href='philippine-war.html'>Philippine War</a> becomes the sharpest political dispute. Washington attempts to preserve its colonial position. Philippine nationalists receive Japanese arms, ships, finance, and recognition. The later Philippine republic enters Japanese association as a sovereign state rather than integral Japanese territory.</p>"},
      {id:"security",title:"Pacific security competition",html:"<p>American planners watch Japanese carriers, submarines, island facilities, and associated-state ports. Japan watches American fleet concentrations, island airfields, corporate penetration, and support for governments along the Pacific rim. Australia and New Zealand remain outside Japanese reach inside the Commonwealth system.</p>"},
      {id:"commerce",title:"Trade and investment",html:"<p>Commercial exchange grows because the states are competitors rather than former total-war enemies. Japanese electronics, vehicles, optics, shipping, and industrial organization meet American capital, consumer markets, aerospace, software, and resource networks. Strategic ownership barriers prevent economic exchange from becoming command dependence.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Space and information systems",html:"<p>Both operate independent stations, lunar systems, launch networks, and communications constellations. Cross-bloc rescue and approach rules reduce accidental escalation. Japanese multilingual standards and American service platforms also meet through international network gateways.</p>"},
      {id:"mature",title:"Relations by 2000",html:"<p>Japan possesses nearly everything its prewar planners sought and has no rational program for conquering America, Australia, or New Zealand. America can contest Japanese expansion without a practical route to dismantling the Asian empire. Their rivalry is therefore intense, maritime, commercial, and normally bounded.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...fivePowerRelationsHub,{href:"imperial-limited-war-directive.html",kicker:"Wartime choice",label:"Imperial Limited-War Directive"},{href:"philippine-war.html",kicker:"Principal postwar conflict",label:"Philippine War"},{href:"japanese-american-investment-boom.html",kicker:"Commercial opening",label:"Japanese–American Investment Boom"}],
    facts:[["General war","Avoided"],["Pearl Harbor attack","Does not occur"],["American occupation of Japan","None"],["Principal postwar dispute","Philippines and Pacific perimeter"],["American Pacific possessions","Retained"],["Formal alliance","None"],["Commercial relationship","Large and strategically screened"],["Status by 2000","Principal Pacific competitors"]]
  }),

  "japan-united-kingdom-relations":fivePowerRelationsArticle({
    title:"Japan–United Kingdom relations",eyebrow:"Anglo–Japanese relations · Asian defeat and maritime accommodation",infoboxKicker:"Singapore, Hong Kong, Burma, the Commonwealth Pacific, and postwar restraint",
    lead:"Japan–United Kingdom relations are the diplomatic, military, commercial, and historical relations between the Empire of Japan and the United Kingdom at the center of the British Commonwealth. Unlike Japan and America, the two states fight a long and destructive war. Japanese victories in Malaya, Singapore, Hong Kong, Burma, and the Indian Ocean become permanent components of the post-Lisbon Asian order and enduring sources of British grievance.",
    canon:"Prewar diplomatic decline, wartime Japanese attack on British Asian possessions, fall of Singapore, Japanese rule in Hong Kong, loss of Burma and much of Britain's Asian position, postwar Commonwealth containment from India and the Pacific, continuing Australian and New Zealand security concerns, practical commercial contact, orbital safety cooperation, and no alliance are established. Exact peace provisions, reparations, embassy chronology, trade totals, and all later maritime crises remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"before",title:"Before the long war",html:"<p>The earlier Anglo–Japanese alliance belongs to a vanished diplomatic order. By 1941 British imperial defense, Japanese war in China, and competition over Southeast Asia place the states on opposing sides despite continuing commercial and diplomatic contacts.</p>"},
      {id:"war",title:"War in Asia",html:"<p>Japan attacks British positions while avoiding American territory. The <a href='fall-of-singapore.html'>fall of Singapore</a>, occupation of <a href='hong-kong-under-japanese-rule.html'>Hong Kong</a>, campaigns in Malaya and Burma, and pressure across the Indian Ocean destroy the assumption that the Royal Navy can restore the Asian empire at will.</p>"},
      {id:"lisbon",title:"Post-Lisbon settlement",html:"<p>Britain accepts the loss of core Asian positions, though Japan never occupies the British Isles. Japan treats its gains as the legitimate liberation and reorganization of Asia. British public memory treats them as defeat, captivity, dispossession, and imperial rupture.</p>"},
      {id:"commonwealth",title:"Commonwealth containment",html:"<p>Australia and New Zealand build defense systems around the possibility of renewed Japanese expansion. Britain retains Indian Ocean and southern routes, while Canada and the Pacific dominions insist that London cannot trade their security for accommodations elsewhere.</p>"},
      {id:"india",title:"India and Burma",html:"<p>British, Japanese, and local successor interests overlap across India, Burma, the Bay of Bengal, and the approaches to Malaya. Arms, transport, recognition, refugees, and development finance matter more often than direct Anglo–Japanese deployments.</p>"},
      {id:"commerce",title:"Commercial accommodation",html:"<p>Shipping, insurance, specialized machinery, consumer goods, finance, and technical standards produce practical contacts. Neither side permits foreign commercial access to become control of strategic ports, communications, launch systems, or defense production.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Orbital and rescue relations",html:"<p>Independent British and Japanese space systems meet under common approach, rescue, docking, and debris rules. Cooperation protects crews and navigation without resolving terrestrial grievances or creating a shared command.</p>"},
      {id:"memory",title:"Memory and mature relations",html:"<p>By 2000 Britain no longer possesses a practical project to reconquer the Japanese sphere, while Japan has no usable reason to attack Australia or New Zealand. War memory remains harsher than daily diplomacy, producing a relationship that is functional, guarded, and emotionally unreconciled.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...fivePowerRelationsHub,{href:"fall-of-singapore.html",kicker:"Defining defeat",label:"Fall of Singapore"},{href:"hong-kong-under-japanese-rule.html",kicker:"Continuing occupation",label:"Hong Kong under Japanese Rule"},{href:"burma.html",kicker:"Continental frontier",label:"Union of Burma"}],
    facts:[["Wartime relationship","Enemies"],["Defining British defeat","Fall of Singapore"],["Major lost positions","Malaya · Singapore · Hong Kong · Burma"],["Commonwealth Pacific concern","Australia and New Zealand"],["Postwar alliance","None"],["Commercial contact","Present"],["Space safety cooperation","Present"],["Status by 2000","Practical accommodation without historical reconciliation"]]
  }),

  "italy-japan-relations":fivePowerRelationsArticle({
    title:"Italy–Japan relations",eyebrow:"Italian–Japanese relations · distant partners and independent imperial systems",infoboxKicker:"Tripartite alignment without a contested postwar frontier",
    lead:"Italy–Japan relations are the diplomatic, commercial, technical, and wartime relations between the Kingdom of Italy and the Empire of Japan. The states enter the divergence as Tripartite Pact partners and benefit from pressure applied to British imperial routes from opposite ends of Eurasia. Their operations remain separate, and postwar distance prevents the intense frontier rivalry found in Germany's relations with either power.",
    canon:"Tripartite alignment, separate wartime theaters, mutual recognition after Lisbon, independent postwar imperial systems, limited trade and technical contact, separate Latin and Japanese space programs, common participation in international rescue and standards arrangements, and absence of a joint command are established. Trade totals, bilateral treaties, diplomatic visits, cultural programs, company portfolios, and later crises are open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"tripartite",title:"Tripartite relationship",html:"<p>Italy and Japan recognize one another's regional leadership through the <a href='tripartite-pact.html'>Tripartite Pact</a>. Italy fights for the Mediterranean, Suez, and Africa while Japan attacks British and Dutch systems in Asia. There is no Italian–Japanese combined theater command.</p>"},
      {id:"britain",title:"Pressure on the British system",html:"<p>Italian control of Mediterranean routes and Japanese victories in Southeast Asia expose both ends of British imperial communications. The strategic effect is cumulative even when plans, logistics, and command remain separate.</p>"},
      {id:"postwar",title:"Separate imperial orders",html:"<p>Lisbon recognizes both victors. Italy organizes the Latin and Mediterranean system; Japan organizes the Co-Prosperity Sphere. The absence of a common land frontier or principal contested client reduces direct military friction.</p>"},
      {id:"diplomacy",title:"Diplomatic distance",html:"<p>Relations are pragmatic rather than intimate. Each state values the other as an independent great power able to complicate German, British, and American calculations, but neither accepts automatic support for wars outside its own sphere.</p>"},
      {id:"commerce",title:"Trade and industry",html:"<p>Exchange concentrates on shipping, optics, machinery, design, chemicals, consumer goods, electronics, and selected licenses. Protected imperial markets and long transport routes keep direct commerce secondary to each system's internal trade.</p>"},
      {id:"culture",title:"Cultural and religious difference",html:"<p>Italy presents itself through monarchy, Fascism, Catholic institutions, design, and the Latin civilizational idea. Japan presents imperial, Shinto, Asian, and developmental claims. Cultural diplomacy emphasizes prestige and antiquity without requiring ideological equivalence.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Space-age relations",html:"<p>The Latin Space Community and Japanese program compete in launch, stations, robotics, lunar establishment, and deep-space exploration. Common rescue, notification, character, and data standards create a thin cooperative layer across otherwise independent systems.</p>"},
      {id:"mature",title:"Mature relationship",html:"<p>Italy–Japan relations are the least confrontational of the ten Five-Power dyads because neither possesses a core territorial demand against the other. Competition remains real in trade, clients, prestige, and standards, but lacks a central frontier likely to convert routine rivalry into war.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...fivePowerRelationsHub,{href:"tripartite-pact.html",kicker:"Wartime framework",label:"Tripartite Pact"},{href:"latin-space-community.html",kicker:"Italian-led program",label:"Latin Space Community"},{href:"geacps.html",kicker:"Japanese system",label:"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere"}],
    facts:[["Wartime relationship","Tripartite partners"],["Combined command","None"],["Principal theaters","Mediterranean and East Asia"],["Common postwar frontier","None"],["Postwar alliance","None"],["Commercial relationship","Limited and specialized"],["Space relationship","Competition plus common safety rules"],["Mature character","Distant pragmatic relations"]]
  }),

  "united-states-united-kingdom-relations":fivePowerRelationsArticle({
    title:"United States–United Kingdom relations",eyebrow:"Anglo–American relations · shared Atlantic interests and sovereign disagreement",infoboxKicker:"Neutrality, Canada, Falklands, Halifax, and selective partnership",
    lead:"United States–United Kingdom relations are the diplomatic, military, economic, and cultural relations between the United States and the United Kingdom at the center of the British Commonwealth. Shared language, law, finance, technology, and Atlantic geography make cooperation frequent, but American neutrality in the long war prevents the wartime alliance and postwar special relationship known in other histories. Britain approaches Washington as a neighboring great power rather than as the junior partner of a common victory.",
    canon:"American neutrality during Britain's defeat, continuing cultural and commercial contact, Canada's dual Atlantic position, British anger at the 1968 Mid-Atlantic settlement, Falklands-era near confrontation and armed mediation, the 1988 Halifax Understanding, later aerospace, computing, rescue, ASW, and commercial cooperation, and no integrated alliance command are established. Exact diplomatic chronologies, intelligence treaties, trade totals, force access, and later disputes remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"war",title:"American neutrality and British defeat",html:"<p>The United States does not enter the long war. Britain therefore receives no full American alliance, combined command, or shared victory. American commerce and opinion matter, but London cannot treat Washington as the power that will inevitably rescue the British position.</p>"},
      {id:"postwar",title:"Postwar distance",html:"<p>Britain rebuilds through the Commonwealth and sterling system while America turns toward the Hemisphere. Cultural familiarity and trade survive, but strategic planning assumes independent decisions and the possibility of divergent interests.</p>"},
      {id:"canada",title:"Canada between the systems",html:"<p>Canada belongs to the Commonwealth while sharing a continent, markets, air approaches, communications, and defense problems with the United States. Ottawa rejects both automatic American command and a British policy that ignores North American geography.</p>"},
      {id:"atlantic",title:"The 1968 Atlantic humiliation",html:"<p>The German–American Mid-Atlantic Ridge Accord alarms London because two outside powers appear to divide Atlantic strategic geography while treating Britain as an object of settlement. The episode reinforces British insistence on independent great-power status.</p>"},
      {id:"falklands",title:"Falklands crisis",html:"<p>The <a href='falklands-war.html'>Falklands War</a> brings the states closest to direct confrontation. Washington's hemispheric obligations and concern for Argentina conflict with Britain's determination to restore its administration. Armed mediation and bounded rules prevent the crisis from becoming an Anglo–American war.</p>"},
      {id:"halifax",title:"Halifax Understanding",html:"<p>The 1988 <a href='halifax-understanding.html'>Halifax Understanding</a>, negotiated with Canada at the table, coordinates Atlantic antisubmarine warfare, search and rescue, orbital distress and docking, crisis notification, South Atlantic monitoring, and selected commercial access while protecting strategic ownership and Commonwealth preference.</p>"},
      {id:"technical",title:"Technical and commercial partnership",html:"<p>Aerospace migration, computing, standards, medicine, research, investment, and civilian space services create dense practical ties. Cooperation remains contractual and sector-specific rather than an integrated alliance economy.</p>"},
      {id:"mature",title:"Relations by 2000",html:"<p>The states are usually partners when Atlantic safety, trade, rescue, or German pressure align their interests. They remain capable of sharp disagreement when American hemispheric doctrine or British Commonwealth sovereignty is at stake.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...fivePowerRelationsHub,{href:"falklands-war.html",kicker:"Near confrontation",label:"Falklands War"},{href:"halifax-understanding.html",kicker:"1988 framework",label:"Halifax Understanding"},{href:"american-aerospace-migration-to-britain.html",kicker:"Technical exchange",label:"American Aerospace Migration to Britain"}],
    facts:[["Wartime alliance","Does not form"],["Principal intermediary","Canada"],["Major postwar grievance","Mid-Atlantic Ridge Accord · 1968"],["Closest military crisis","Falklands War"],["Controlling later framework","Halifax Understanding · 1988"],["Integrated command","None"],["Commercial relationship","Dense and protected"],["Mature character","Selective partnership between sovereign powers"]]
  }),

  "italy-united-states-relations":fivePowerRelationsArticle({
    title:"Italy–United States relations",eyebrow:"Italian–American relations · commerce, Catholic diplomacy, and hemispheric competition",infoboxKicker:"No wartime collision, but overlapping interests throughout the Americas",
    lead:"Italy–United States relations are the diplomatic, commercial, cultural, and strategic relations between the Kingdom of Italy and the United States. American neutrality prevents a direct wartime conflict with Fascist Italy. Postwar relations become competitive where the Italian-led Latin system reaches into Catholic institutions, migration networks, finance, commerce, and aligned states of the American Hemisphere.",
    canon:"Absence of a direct wartime conflict, postwar American opposition to rival strategic control in the Hemisphere, Latin cultural and commercial influence in Mexico, Cuba, Argentina, and other states, Falklands-era diplomatic tension, later investment growth, monetary and corporate competition, space and standards contact, and no alliance are established. Exact treaties, ambassadors, trade totals, church agreements, intelligence cases, and corporate portfolios remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"war",title:"Neutrality during the Axis war",html:"<p>Italy fights Britain and builds its Mediterranean empire while the United States remains outside the general war. The two states avoid the direct campaigns, occupation, and peace settlement that would otherwise dominate their later relationship.</p>"},
      {id:"hemisphere",title:"The Hemisphere and Latin world",html:"<p>Washington distinguishes ordinary Italian trade and culture from strategic penetration. Rome and its partners distinguish cultural affinity, commercial access, and sovereign invitation from American claims that the Hemisphere constitutes one security system.</p>"},
      {id:"states",title:"Principal national arenas",html:"<p>Mexico uses Latin firms and the scudo system to diversify without becoming an Italian client. Cuba favors Spanish and wider Latin access as a barrier to American corporate integration. Argentina combines Latin connections with German defense influence and American commerce.</p>"},
      {id:"religion",title:"Catholic diplomacy",html:"<p>Church networks, education, charities, migration, publishing, and family politics give the Latin system cultural reach unavailable to a purely military bloc. The Holy See and national churches retain interests distinct from the Italian state, preventing Rome from treating Catholic affinity as automatic obedience.</p>"},
      {id:"falklands",title:"South Atlantic crisis",html:"<p>Argentina's Falklands claim and Britain's response place Washington and the Latin diplomatic world on different sides of a dangerous dispute. Portugal's ability to mediate demonstrates that Latin affiliation does not require every member to repeat one Italian position.</p>"},
      {id:"commerce",title:"Commercial opening",html:"<p>The World Market Boom expands Latin–American investment, shipping, consumer trade, finance, and joint ventures. Strategic ports, communications, energy, aerospace, and defense firms remain protected by national ownership rules.</p>"},
      {id:"money",title:"Monetary relations",html:"<p>The gold dollar and Latin scudo are competing settlement instruments rather than mutually exclusive currencies. Clearing, bullion, bank access, and sovereign reserves allow trade across the blocs while preserving monetary independence.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Space and mature relations",html:"<p>Independent American and Latin space systems compete for prestige, contracts, launch services, and exploration claims while sharing rescue, notification, and data standards. By 2000 rivalry is most intense through companies and associated states rather than direct Italian–American military confrontation.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...fivePowerRelationsHub,{href:"american-hemisphere.html",kicker:"Regional doctrine",label:"American Hemisphere"},{href:"latin-american-investment-boom.html",kicker:"Commercial opening",label:"Latin–American Investment Boom"},{href:"mexican-gold-currency.html",kicker:"Monetary bridge",label:"Mexican Gold Currency"}],
    facts:[["Direct wartime conflict","None"],["Principal postwar theater","American Hemisphere"],["Latin cultural instrument","Catholic and migration networks"],["Principal bridge states","Mexico · Cuba · Argentina"],["Monetary systems","Gold dollar and Latin scudo"],["Formal alliance","None"],["Strategic ownership","Protected"],["Mature character","Commercial competition without direct military rivalry"]]
  }),

  "italy-united-kingdom-relations":fivePowerRelationsArticle({
    title:"Italy–United Kingdom relations",eyebrow:"Anglo–Italian relations · Mediterranean war and postwar route rivalry",infoboxKicker:"Suez, imperial communications, Kuwait, diplomacy, and selective accommodation",
    lead:"Italy–United Kingdom relations are the diplomatic, military, commercial, and imperial relations between the Kingdom of Italy and the United Kingdom at the center of the British Commonwealth. The two states fight for the Mediterranean, North Africa, Suez, and imperial communications during the long war. Italian victory permanently removes Britain from much of its former central Mediterranean and Middle Eastern position without eliminating British naval reach or Commonwealth interests.",
    canon:"Wartime enmity, Italian victory in the Mediterranean and Suez, British loss of regional primacy, continuing competition over routes and clients, indirect Latin assistance during the Kuwait crisis, Portuguese mediation in the Falklands settlement, later commerce and space rules, and absence of an alliance are established. Exact peace clauses, reparations, embassy chronology, trade totals, naval incidents, and all bilateral agreements remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"war",title:"Mediterranean war",html:"<p>Italy and Britain fight across North Africa, the central and eastern Mediterranean, the Red Sea approaches, and imperial routes. Italian advances through Egypt and control of Suez break a central assumption of British global communications.</p>"},
      {id:"lisbon",title:"Post-Lisbon order",html:"<p>Lisbon recognizes Italian gains while preserving an unoccupied Britain and functioning Royal Navy. Rome treats the Mediterranean settlement as a completed imperial victory. London treats it as a loss to be contained, bypassed, or offset through the Cape and Commonwealth routes.</p>"},
      {id:"routes",title:"Routes and imperial systems",html:"<p>Italian influence at Suez, North Africa, the Red Sea, and the eastern Mediterranean intersects with British interests in the Gulf, Cape system, Indian Ocean, and surviving Commonwealth possessions. Shipping rights, tolls, port access, intelligence, and client politics create recurring friction below the threshold of war.</p>"},
      {id:"kuwait",title:"Kuwait and bounded intervention",html:"<p>During the <a href='kuwait-war.html'>Kuwait War</a>, Britain leads the coalition response while Latin governments assist indirectly. Italy avoids converting support for regional balance into a direct war with Britain, demonstrating that Mediterranean rivalry can coexist with a common interest in preventing uncontrolled conquest.</p>"},
      {id:"portugal",title:"The Portuguese bridge",html:"<p>Portugal retains old British connections while participating in the Latin diplomatic world. Its mediation during the Falklands crisis gives both systems a channel neither Washington nor Berlin controls and shows that Latin institutions do not erase national diplomatic traditions.</p>"},
      {id:"commerce",title:"Commerce and finance",html:"<p>Shipping, insurance, machinery, tourism, design, energy, and imperial transit support practical accommodation. Britain protects sterling and Commonwealth preference; Italy protects the scudo area, strategic holding companies, and its Mediterranean concessions.</p>"},
      {id:"space",title:"Space-age contact",html:"<p>British and Latin stations, lunar establishments, navigation systems, and exploration programs compete for prestige and contracts. Rescue duties, docking compatibility, debris warnings, and common scientific practice supply a cooperative layer not available during the war generation.</p>"},
      {id:"mature",title:"Relations by 2000",html:"<p>Neither power accepts the other's preferred history of Mediterranean legitimacy. Both nevertheless benefit from open routes, localized crisis settlements, and the recognition that a general Anglo–Italian war would damage the very imperial and commercial systems each seeks to preserve.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[...fivePowerRelationsHub,{href:"mediterranean-and-middle-east-campaign.html",kicker:"Wartime theater",label:"Mediterranean and Middle East Campaign"},{href:"kuwait-war.html",kicker:"Postwar intervention",label:"Kuwait War"},{href:"falklands-lisbon-talks.html",kicker:"Portuguese mediation",label:"Falklands Lisbon Talks"}],
    facts:[["Wartime relationship","Enemies"],["Principal wartime theater","Mediterranean · North Africa · Suez"],["Postwar route dispute","Suez versus Cape and Commonwealth systems"],["Major limited-war contact","Kuwait War"],["Diplomatic bridge","Portugal"],["Formal alliance","None"],["Commercial relationship","Practical and protected"],["Mature character","Route rivalry with selective accommodation"]]
  })
});

const addFivePowerRelationsRelated=(slug,item)=>{
  const article=window.deepArticles[slug];
  if(!article)return;
  article.related=article.related||[];
  if(!article.related.some(link=>link.href===item.href))article.related.push(item);
};

